{"attack": true, "case": "The claim purports to defend Popper's preference for *better-corroborated* theories, but its actual defense mechanism appeals to *testability* — a distinct a priori property in Popper's framework. The claim's own text frames the defense as selecting 'the most \"testable\" (and thus most informative) target for future falsification,' while the mechanism shifts to 'preferring a severely tested theory' that 'has survived the hardest attempts to kill it.' These are not equivalent: testability is a function of logical content (a theory can be maximally testable the moment it is proposed, with zero track record), whereas corroboration is an a posteriori property of having survived severe tests. By grounding the defense in testability rather than corroboration, the argument fails to justify the actual Popperian preference it claims to defend — why prefer a theory that has *already survived* tests over an equally testable but as-yet-untested competitor. If testability is doing the justificatory work, corroboration is irrelevant to the preference; if corroboration (past survival) is doing the work, the testability framing is a non-sequitur and the defense must explain why past survival matters without smuggling in exactly the kind of inductive warrant the counterconditions forbid. The conflation evades rather than resolves the central problem.", "counterexample": ["Consider two theories, T1 and T2, both maximally testable (same informational content). T1 has passed 1000 severe tests; T2 was proposed yesterday and has passed none. Popper's preference for *better-corroborated* theories says prefer T1. But the claim's defense — appealing to testability as 'most informative target for future falsification' — gives identical marks to T1 and T2, since they are equally testable. The defense therefore cannot generate the preference it claims to defend."], "premise": "In Popper's framework, testability (logical content/informativeness) and corroboration (having survived severe tests) are distinct properties; conflating them leaves the stated target — preference for better-corroborated theories — undefended.", "premise_evidence": [{"block": "3c725ad440c3283cf95a34b094777be8e98156763caf092a1d520f5d958a22c7", "quote": "as a methodological rule for selecting the most 'testable' (and thus most informative) target for future falsification"}, {"block": "3c725ad440c3283cf95a34b094777be8e98156763caf092a1d520f5d958a22c7", "quote": "preferring a severely tested theory is equivalent to preferring the theory that has survived the hardest attempts to kill it, thereby offering the highest potential for learning if it is eventually false"}], "successor_question": "Can a purely methodological (non-inductive) defense of Popper's preference for *better-corroborated* theories — as opposed to merely more *testable* theories — explain why a theory's past survival of severe tests should rationally guide present preference, without that explanation collapsing into either irrelevance (testability alone) or disguised induction (past performance predicting future reliability)?"}